            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces each value of an array with the running total up to and including it: the first value, then the first two added, then the first three, and so on.\n\nA sum gives you the total; a cumulative sum gives you the total SO FAR at every point. Use it to turn steps into a path (movements into positions, speeds into distance travelled), to turn a histogram into a cumulative distribution, or to turn a list of durations into start times.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cumsum <axis: int>\nDefault axis -1, the last axis: along each row.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.cumsum\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is summed and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. A single number has no axis to run along, and sends nothing.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to run. For a 2-D array, -1 or 1 runs along each row on its own, 0 or -2 runs down each column. Negative counts from the end. An axis the array does not have sends nothing. Changing it does not send by itself; the next array uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncumsum:\nThe running totals, the same shape as the input. The last value is the sum of the whole row (what np.sum would give).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: np.rand makes 200 random steps between -1 and 1, a new set every half second. The left plot shows them - noise, with no shape. The right plot is their cumsum: a random walk, the path you get by taking those steps one after another. Drag np.rand's max up to 1.2 (or min down) and the steps are no longer balanced: the walk drifts steadily up (or down).\n\nBottom: ten 1s summed give the ramp 1 2 3 ... 10 - the cumsum of a constant is a straight line whose slope is the constant. Edit the message (2 2 2, or 1 -1 1 -1) and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diff is the reverse: it turns a path back into its steps. np.cumprod multiplies instead of adding. accumulate keeps a running total of a STREAM, one number at a time. np.sum gives only the final total. t.cumsum is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces each value of an array with the running total up to and including it: the first value, then the first two added, then the first three, and so on.\n\nA sum gives you the total; a cumulative sum gives you the total SO FAR at every point. Use it to turn steps into a path (movements into positions, speeds into distance travelled), to turn a histogram into a cumulative distribution, or to turn a list of durations into start times.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cumsum <axis: int>\nDefault axis -1, the last axis: along each row.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.cumsum\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is summed and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. A single number has no axis to run along, and sends nothing.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to run. For a 2-D array, -1 or 1 runs along each row on its own, 0 or -2 runs down each column. Negative counts from the end. An axis the array does not have sends nothing. Changing it does not send by itself; the next array uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncumsum:\nThe running totals, the same shape as the input. The last value is the sum of the whole row (what np.sum would give).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: np.rand makes 200 random steps between -1 and 1, a new set every half second. The left plot shows them - noise, with no shape. The right plot is their cumsum: a random walk, the path you get by taking those steps one after another. Drag np.rand's max up to 1.2 (or min down) and the steps are no longer balanced: the walk drifts steadily up (or down).\n\nBottom: ten 1s summed give the ramp 1 2 3 ... 10 - the cumsum of a constant is a straight line whose slope is the constant. Edit the message (2 2 2, or 1 -1 1 -1) and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diff is the reverse: it turns a path back into its steps. np.cumprod multiplies instead of adding. accumulate keeps a running total of a STREAM, one number at a time. np.sum gives only the final total. t.cumsum is the PyTorch version."
        },
